1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,132
You work in a call centre.

2
00:00:02,132 --> 00:00:07,115
Eight hours a day in a chair, with a
headset on, for just above minimum wage.

3
00:00:07,427 --> 00:00:11,804
It pays the rent. Most months.
It does not pay for much else.

4
00:00:12,158 --> 00:00:14,932
There is a credit card
you try not to look at.

5
00:00:14,932 --> 00:00:18,006
Four thousand dollars, and
the number only goes up.

6
00:00:18,534 --> 00:00:20,364
Lunch comes from a vending machine.

7
00:00:20,364 --> 00:00:23,031
Dinner comes from whatever
is open at ten at night.

8
00:00:23,425 --> 00:00:28,817
You are not lazy. You are tired. There is
a difference, and nobody seems to see it.

9
00:00:29,397 --> 00:00:32,811
Somewhere along the way, the
hoodie stopped being a style.

10
00:00:32,811 --> 00:00:34,283
It became a hiding place.

11
00:00:34,588 --> 00:00:37,798
You stopped going to the beach.
You stopped going in photos.

12
00:00:38,127 --> 00:00:40,503
You tell yourself you
will start on Monday.

13
00:00:40,503 --> 00:00:42,658
You have said that on a lot of Mondays.

14
00:00:42,962 --> 00:00:44,830
You even bought running shoes once.

15
00:00:44,830 --> 00:00:48,246
They are still in the box, under
the bed, with the price tag on.

16
00:00:48,821 --> 00:00:52,239
At family dinner, your mom puts
salad on your plate without a word.

17
00:00:52,611 --> 00:00:55,997
Dex, who has been going to the
gym since school, has advice.

18
00:00:56,273 --> 00:00:59,478
"Bro, just eat less and move
more. It's not that deep."

19
00:01:00,014 --> 00:01:04,441
Everyone has a simple answer.
Nobody has to live in your body.

20
00:01:04,591 --> 00:01:08,345
Then, at a routine check-up,
the doctor goes quiet.

21
00:01:08,586 --> 00:01:13,613
Your blood pressure is high. Not dangerous
yet, she says. But heading there.

22
00:01:14,045 --> 00:01:18,923
"You need to start moving. A little
every day. Not someday. Now."

23
00:01:19,192 --> 00:01:23,014
So you do what everybody does. You
go to the big gym on the corner.

24
00:01:23,288 --> 00:01:27,219
The manager is called Brick. He looks
like he was built in a factory.

25
00:01:27,498 --> 00:01:31,657
Sixty dollars a month. Twelve-month
contract. Joining fee on top.

26
00:01:31,976 --> 00:01:35,443
You ask if there is any way to
pay a little less, just to start.

27
00:01:35,617 --> 00:01:39,914
Brick laughs. "People like you sign
up in January and quit in February.

28
00:01:39,914 --> 00:01:42,215
That's why we take the money upfront."

29
00:01:42,470 --> 00:01:46,402
You walk out past a wall of mirrors.
You do not look in any of them.

30
00:01:46,621 --> 00:01:51,699
Your bank balance says three hundred and
twelve dollars. Rent is due in nine days.

31
00:01:51,930 --> 00:01:56,708
You cannot afford the gym. And according
to your doctor, you cannot afford to wait.

32
00:01:56,938 --> 00:01:59,161
And that is when you see it clearly.

33
00:01:59,161 --> 00:02:02,865
Your body and your bank account
are stuck in the same place.

34
00:02:03,111 --> 00:02:06,580
On the walk home, you
pass a bus stop advert.

35
00:02:06,580 --> 00:02:10,665
A man with a perfect body,
and two words. No excuses.

36
00:02:10,897 --> 00:02:16,497
You have plenty of excuses. Every one
of them is real. That is the problem.

37
00:02:16,647 --> 00:02:20,985
So you find a free workout video
online. Thirty minutes. Full body.

38
00:02:21,344 --> 00:02:22,818
You make it four minutes.

39
00:02:23,102 --> 00:02:27,681
The next day, you cannot walk down the
stairs without holding the rail. Again.

40
00:02:28,142 --> 00:02:32,788
You try again on Thursday. Then
the next Monday. Then you stop.

41
00:02:33,335 --> 00:02:36,977
Each time you stop, you feel a little
worse than before you started.

42
00:02:37,282 --> 00:02:42,454
Every plan you find is made by people who
already love exercise, for people who

43
00:02:42,454 --> 00:02:43,894
already love exercise.

44
00:02:44,173 --> 00:02:47,150
Nobody makes a plan for someone
who is starting from the floor.

45
00:02:47,498 --> 00:02:52,243
And lying there, you have an idea that
sounds almost too small to matter.

46
00:02:52,393 --> 00:02:56,061
What if you made the plan so
easy that you could not fail it?

47
00:02:56,451 --> 00:03:00,703
Day one. Ten squats, holding the
kitchen counter. That is it.

48
00:03:00,982 --> 00:03:05,356
It feels ridiculous. You do it anyway,
and you put a tick in the notebook.

49
00:03:05,580 --> 00:03:10,142
Day two. Ten squats and a five-minute
walk around the block. Tick.

50
00:03:10,392 --> 00:03:13,748
You add one small thing
each week. Never two.

51
00:03:13,990 --> 00:03:17,673
Wall push-ups. Then stairs,
once, slowly. Then twice.

52
00:03:18,034 --> 00:03:23,011
You swap the vending machine for a banana
and a sandwich from home. Not every day.

53
00:03:23,011 --> 00:03:23,618
Most days.

54
00:03:23,823 --> 00:03:28,446
Some weeks you miss three days in a
row. The old you would have quit.

55
00:03:28,446 --> 00:03:31,730
This time, you just start
again on the next page.

56
00:03:31,918 --> 00:03:37,517
You write down everything. What you did.
How you felt. What hurt. What helped.

57
00:03:37,696 --> 00:03:42,419
Without knowing it, you are writing a map.
The map you wished someone had given you.

58
00:03:42,788 --> 00:03:46,803
Week six. You climb all fourteen
stairs without stopping.

59
00:03:47,111 --> 00:03:52,701
Nobody claps. Nobody is there. But you
stand at the top for a long time anyway.

60
00:03:52,851 --> 00:03:56,177
Month three. The doctor checks
your blood pressure again.

61
00:03:56,466 --> 00:04:00,554
She looks at the number twice.
"Whatever you're doing, keep doing it."

62
00:04:00,777 --> 00:04:05,872
You have lost six kilos. Slowly. The
way the doctor said it should happen.

63
00:04:06,132 --> 00:04:10,808
Your hoodie is loose. You take it off at
work for the first time in two years.

64
00:04:10,885 --> 00:04:15,275
A coworker you have sat next to for two
years says, "Have you done something

65
00:04:15,275 --> 00:04:16,950
different?" You nearly laugh.

66
00:04:17,209 --> 00:04:20,545
Then Sam from the next desk asks
you, quietly, how you did it.

67
00:04:20,595 --> 00:04:25,754
He cannot afford the gym either. He
works nights at a hospital as well.

68
00:04:25,754 --> 00:04:27,062
Two jobs, no time.

69
00:04:27,291 --> 00:04:31,324
You photocopy your notebook for
him. The whole plan, week by week.

70
00:04:31,441 --> 00:04:36,346
Six weeks later, Sam sends you a message.
"I did the stairs. All of them."

71
00:04:36,580 --> 00:04:39,831
And then his sister asks for
a copy. And then her friend.

72
00:04:39,979 --> 00:04:43,514
And that is when you realise the
notebook is not just yours anymore.

73
00:04:43,641 --> 00:04:48,075
Something you made out of being broke and
out of breath might be worth something to

74
00:04:48,075 --> 00:04:48,770
someone else.

75
00:04:48,920 --> 00:04:53,680
You turn it into a clean, simple
guide. Twelve weeks. No equipment.

76
00:04:53,680 --> 00:04:55,243
Fifteen minutes a day.

77
00:04:55,602 --> 00:04:59,391
Every week starts with one thing
so easy it is almost funny.

78
00:04:59,680 --> 00:05:02,686
You call it The Living
Room Plan. Nine dollars.

79
00:05:02,686 --> 00:05:04,924
For people who cannot afford a gym.

80
00:05:05,221 --> 00:05:08,127
You put it on a simple shop
page, and press publish.

81
00:05:08,344 --> 00:05:12,178
One sale. It is Sam's sister.
She insisted on paying.

82
00:05:12,422 --> 00:05:14,188
Then nothing for two weeks.

83
00:05:14,267 --> 00:05:19,224
Dex sees the page and sends a laughing
face. "Bro is a fitness influencer now."

84
00:05:19,274 --> 00:05:22,721
And the old voice comes back.
Who are you to teach anybody?

85
00:05:22,721 --> 00:05:24,473
You were the guy on the floor.

86
00:05:24,916 --> 00:05:29,714
But that is exactly the point.
You were the guy on the floor.

87
00:05:29,864 --> 00:05:32,225
So you stop hiding who the plan is for.

88
00:05:32,308 --> 00:05:36,282
You film a fifteen-second video.
Day one of your old notebook.

89
00:05:36,282 --> 00:05:38,461
Ten squats at the kitchen counter.

90
00:05:38,601 --> 00:05:43,678
No music. No six-pack. Just you,
out of breath, telling the truth.

91
00:05:43,975 --> 00:05:48,042
You post one every day. Your
actual old days, in order.

92
00:05:48,322 --> 00:05:52,408
The views are tiny. Forty.
Sixty. You keep going.

93
00:05:52,652 --> 00:05:56,397
You almost stop on day eleven.
Then a single comment appears.

94
00:05:56,397 --> 00:05:58,484
"Doing day one with you tomorrow."

95
00:05:58,717 --> 00:06:02,148
Then on day nineteen, you post
the stairs. All fourteen.

96
00:06:02,148 --> 00:06:04,598
Still a little out of breath at the top.

97
00:06:04,861 --> 00:06:07,582
You wake up to two hundred thousand views.

98
00:06:07,722 --> 00:06:11,021
The comments are not about
muscles. They are about stairs.

99
00:06:11,230 --> 00:06:15,249
People who have never felt welcome in a
gym, finally seeing somebody who looks

100
00:06:15,249 --> 00:06:15,764
like them.

101
00:06:15,814 --> 00:06:19,141
By the end of the week, the counter
says four hundred and twelve.

102
00:06:19,624 --> 00:06:25,271
Then the first hard message. "I did week
one and my knees hurt. Is this even safe?"

103
00:06:25,500 --> 00:06:26,530
You do not guess.

104
00:06:26,530 --> 00:06:30,046
You pay a physiotherapist to
check every week of the plan.

105
00:06:30,270 --> 00:06:34,387
You add easier versions of every move, and
a page that says: if it hurts, stop, and

106
00:06:34,387 --> 00:06:35,329
ask a professional.

107
00:06:35,379 --> 00:06:39,753
Because the people buying this are
trusting you with their bodies.

108
00:06:39,753 --> 00:06:41,807
That is not something you rush.

109
00:06:41,961 --> 00:06:46,173
You send the update free to every buyer,
with a note saying sorry it was not there

110
00:06:46,173 --> 00:06:46,943
from the start.

111
00:06:47,235 --> 00:06:51,340
That update gets you more five-star
reviews than the original plan ever did.

112
00:06:51,511 --> 00:06:56,049
People ask for more. So you add a
walking plan. Then a cheap meal guide.

113
00:06:56,049 --> 00:06:58,697
Each one from a question in your comments.

114
00:06:58,801 --> 00:07:02,931
Nine dollars at a time, the side project
starts paying more than the call centre.

115
00:07:03,135 --> 00:07:07,492
The credit card gets paid off first. Then
the rent stops being a monthly panic.

116
00:07:07,782 --> 00:07:10,531
And one morning, you
hand back the headset.

117
00:07:10,681 --> 00:07:15,788
One year after you couldn't climb fourteen
stairs, thirty thousand people have used

118
00:07:15,788 --> 00:07:16,403
your plan.

119
00:07:16,768 --> 00:07:19,337
You have lost eighteen kilos. Slowly.

120
00:07:19,337 --> 00:07:23,713
Your doctor high-fives you, which
you did not know doctors did.

121
00:07:24,022 --> 00:07:25,553
The hoodie goes into a drawer.

122
00:07:25,841 --> 00:07:30,036
You hire Sam to answer the messages. He
still does the stairs every morning.

123
00:07:30,373 --> 00:07:34,375
And you take your mom on a walk along
the beach. The one you stopped going to.

124
00:07:34,862 --> 00:07:38,754
She does not say anything. She just
holds your arm a little tighter.

125
00:07:38,904 --> 00:07:40,733
And then, they come back.

126
00:07:40,943 --> 00:07:43,169
Dex hurts his back lifting too heavy.

127
00:07:43,169 --> 00:07:46,297
His physio tells him to
start again from the basics.

128
00:07:46,529 --> 00:07:49,360
The page the physio shows
him is from your plan.

129
00:07:49,601 --> 00:07:52,627
And then an email arrives from
the big gym on the corner.

130
00:07:52,708 --> 00:07:56,538
Their members keep quitting in February.
They want a beginner programme.

131
00:07:56,538 --> 00:07:59,516
They have heard yours is the
one people actually finish.

132
00:07:59,786 --> 00:08:01,908
You walk back through the glass doors.

133
00:08:01,908 --> 00:08:04,477
Brick is at the desk,
with the same clipboard.

134
00:08:04,706 --> 00:08:05,986
He does not recognise you.

135
00:08:06,358 --> 00:08:09,858
You put a single page on the
counter. Your old gym quote.

136
00:08:09,858 --> 00:08:12,622
Sixty dollars a month,
twelve-month contract.

137
00:08:12,808 --> 00:08:14,671
"I couldn't afford this," you say.

138
00:08:14,671 --> 00:08:17,522
"So I built something for
the people you turn away."

139
00:08:17,800 --> 00:08:20,251
Then you sit down, and
you name your price.

140
00:08:20,530 --> 00:08:24,034
He reads the number twice. Then he signs.

141
00:08:24,184 --> 00:08:28,301
You did not need a gym. You needed
a plan small enough to start.

142
00:08:28,613 --> 00:08:32,384
And the thing that helped you is the thing
someone else is still searching for.

143
00:08:32,760 --> 00:08:36,748
You fixed your body, and the thing you
built while fixing it fixed your bank

144
00:08:36,748 --> 00:08:37,168
account.

145
00:08:37,496 --> 00:08:39,513
That is what a digital product is.

146
00:08:39,513 --> 00:08:43,311
Something you lived, turned into
something someone else can use.

147
00:08:43,549 --> 00:08:48,237
If you want to learn how to turn what you
know into your first digital product, the

148
00:08:48,237 --> 00:08:51,287
step-by-step guide is linked
in my bio. And subscribe.

149
00:08:51,287 --> 00:08:54,112
Because next week, somebody
else starts from zero.
